<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
    <head>
        <meta charset="utf-8"/>
        <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"/>
        <meta name="theme-color" content="#111316"/>
        <title>RISC-V Studio — Interactive 3D Processor Simulator</title>
    </head>
    <body>
        <div id="root"></div>

        <div id="help-message" class="d-none">
            <p>
                Welcome to <b>RISC&#8209;V Studio</b>, an interactive 3D processor laboratory for a 32&#8209;bit, single-cycle RISC&#8209;V datapath.
                All 32-bit integer instructions are supported except the syscall and concurrency related instructions. The datapath is
                closely based on the design described in <i>Computer Organization and Design: RISC&#8209;V Edition</i>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Write RISC&#8209;V assembly in the left editor, set initial registers and data memory, then press Play or Step. The centre view
                is a live 3D model of the datapath: active data paths glow cyan, control signals orange, branch and jump paths purple and
                register write-back green. Drag to orbit, scroll to zoom, and click any component to inspect its purpose, live inputs and
                outputs, and its role in the current instruction. The right inspector shows registers, memory, the decoded instruction and
                every control signal, together with a step-by-step explanation of the executing instruction.
            </p>
            <p>
                Use the <b>Examples</b> menu to load one of the bundled programs, and the import/export buttons to save a complete setup as
                JSON. Radix and word-size choices apply to both the editors and the inspector. Use the <code>ebreak</code> instruction to
                insert a breakpoint in the simulation.
            </p>
            <p>
                You can view the source or contribute on <a href="https://github.com/alokmuskan/Risc-V-Graphical-Simulator" target="_blank">GitHub</a>.
            </p>
        </div>
    </body>
</html>